## sp-sol | The solar system | physics | 0 | - | x-light
explain 3 | The Sun holds 99.8% of the solar system's mass
- Jupiter holds most of the rest: more than twice all the other planets combined.
- The centre of mass of the Sun and Jupiter lies just outside the Sun's surface.
- *So the Sun wobbles, which is one way we detect planets around other stars.*
> Venus is hotter than Mercury

quiz 3 | Which planet has the hottest surface?
* Venus
o Mercury
o Mars
o Jupiter
= About 465°C, hot enough to melt lead, from a runaway greenhouse effect under thick CO₂.
= Mercury is closer to the Sun but has no atmosphere to trap heat.
> A day on Venus is longer than its year

explain 4 | Venus spins backwards, very slowly
- It rotates once every 243 Earth days, in the opposite direction to most planets.
- It orbits the Sun in 225 days.
- *From the surface, the Sun would rise in the west.*
> Saturn would float

myth 3 T | Saturn's average density is less than water's.
= About 0.69 g/cm³. In a big enough bathtub, it would (theoretically) float.
= Its rings are mostly water ice, and less than 1 km thick in most places.
> Pluto lost its status in 2006

story 4 | Why Pluto was demoted
- 2005: Eris was found, about as big as Pluto, with more surely to come.
- The IAU defined a planet in 2006: it must have cleared its orbital neighbourhood. Pluto hasn't.
- *New Horizons flew past in 2015 and found mountains of water ice and glaciers of nitrogen.*
> Mars has the tallest volcano known

quiz 3 | Olympus Mons on Mars is about how tall?
* About 22 km
o About 9 km
o About 50 km
o About 2 km
= Roughly two and a half times Everest's height above sea level.
= Mars's low gravity and lack of moving plates let lava pile up in one place.
> Europa may have more water than Earth

explain 4 | Ocean moons
- Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's Enceladus have liquid water oceans under ice.
- Enceladus shoots geysers of water into space; Cassini flew through them.
- *They're among the best places to look for life beyond Earth.*
> Next: stars

## sp-star | Stars | physics | 0 | - | sp-sol
explain 3 | Stars are fusion reactors held together by gravity
- Gravity squeezes inward; pressure from fusion heat pushes outward.
- The Sun fuses about 600 million tonnes of hydrogen per second.
- *It has enough fuel for about another 5 billion years.*
> Bigger stars live shorter lives

quiz 3 | Which lives longest?
* A red dwarf with 0.2 solar masses
o The Sun
o A blue giant with 20 solar masses
o They all live about 10 billion years
= Red dwarfs burn so slowly they can last trillions of years: longer than the universe has existed.
= Massive stars burn out in a few million years.
> Light from the Sun's core takes ages to escape

explain 4 | Sunlight is old
- A photon made in the Sun's core is absorbed and re-emitted countless times.
- It takes on the order of 100,000 years (estimates vary widely) to random-walk out.
- *Then just 8 minutes to reach you.*
> You're made of star stuff

explain 4 | Where the elements came from
- Hydrogen and most helium: the Big Bang.
- Carbon, oxygen and up to iron: fusion in stars.
- *Heavier elements: supernovae and neutron star mergers.* The calcium in your bones was made in stars.
> Betelgeuse dimmed in 2019

story 3 | The Great Dimming of Betelgeuse
- Late 2019, the red supergiant Betelgeuse dimmed dramatically. People wondered if it was about to explode.
- Observations showed a dust cloud from material it had ejected.
- *It will go supernova someday, but probably not for about 100,000 years.*
> Next: galaxies

## sp-gal | Galaxies | physics | 0 | - | sp-star
explain 3 | The Milky Way has 100–400 billion stars
- We're about 26,000 light-years from its centre, in a spiral arm.
- At its centre is a black hole of about 4 million solar masses: Sagittarius A*.
- *Its image was released in 2022 by the Event Horizon Telescope.*
> Andromeda will collide with us

explain 3 | Andromeda is heading our way
- The Andromeda galaxy approaches at about 110 km/s.
- In roughly 4–5 billion years, the two galaxies will merge.
- *Stars are so far apart that almost none will actually collide.*
> Edwin Hubble showed galaxies exist

story 5 | Before 1924, the Milky Way was thought to be the whole universe
- Spiral "nebulae" were debated: gas clouds within our galaxy, or galaxies of their own?
- Edwin Hubble measured Cepheid variable stars in Andromeda and showed it was far outside the Milky Way.
- *The universe got unimaginably bigger overnight.*
> Henrietta Leavitt made it possible

story 4 | Henrietta Leavitt's ruler for the universe
- Working at Harvard as a "computer" in the 1900s–1910s, she found Cepheid stars' brightness pulses relate to their true luminosity.
- That made them standard candles for measuring distance.
- *Hubble's discoveries were built on her law.*
> Next: planets around other stars

## sp-exo | Exoplanets | physics | 0 | - | sp-gal
explain 3 | Over 5,000 exoplanets are confirmed
- The first around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, was found in 1995: a gas giant orbiting in 4 days.
- Most are found by the transit method: a tiny dip as a planet crosses its star.
- *Mayor and Queloz won the 2019 Nobel Prize for 51 Peg b.*
> Hot Jupiters surprised everyone

explain 4 | The habitable zone
- The range of distances where liquid water could exist on a planet's surface.
- TRAPPIST-1, a red dwarf 40 light-years away, has seven Earth-sized planets, several in this zone.
- *Habitable zone doesn't mean inhabited.*
> JWST reads alien atmospheres

real 4 | The James Webb telescope reads exoplanet atmospheres
- As a planet transits, starlight filters through its atmosphere. Molecules absorb specific wavelengths.
- JWST detected carbon dioxide in an exoplanet atmosphere in 2022.
- *We're starting to check alien skies for the chemistry of life.*
> The Fermi paradox

explain 4 | The Fermi paradox: where is everybody?
- Billions of stars, many with planets, and billions of years for civilisations to spread.
- Yet no evidence of anyone.
- *Possible answers: life is rare, civilisations self-destruct, they're quiet, or we're not looking right.*
> Next: space exploration

## sp-miss | Space missions | physics | 0 | - | sp-exo
story 5 | Voyager carries a golden record
- Voyager 1 and 2, launched in 1977, each carry a gold-plated record of sounds and images from Earth.
- Greetings in 55 languages, music from Bach to Chuck Berry, and a diagram showing how to play it.
- *Voyager 1 entered interstellar space in 2012.*
> The Pale Blue Dot

story 4 | The Pale Blue Dot
- 1990: Carl Sagan persuaded NASA to turn Voyager 1's camera back toward Earth from about 6 billion km away.
- Earth appears as a speck less than a pixel wide in a sunbeam.
- *"That's here. That's home. That's us."*
> Hubble launched with a blurry mirror

story 4 | Hubble was launched short-sighted
- 1990: Hubble's main mirror had been ground to the wrong shape by about 2 micrometres.
- Astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993, like glasses for a telescope.
- *It went on to become one of the most productive scientific instruments ever built.*
> Rovers on Mars

quiz 3 | Opportunity was designed to last 90 Martian days. How long did it actually operate?
* About 15 years
o About 6 months
o About 3 years
o About 40 years
= It landed in 2004 and last communicated in 2018, before a dust storm covered its solar panels.
> Space track complete

## mt-prime | Primes | maths | 0 | - | m-inf
story 4 | Euclid proved there are infinitely many primes
- Suppose there were finitely many. Multiply them all and add 1.
- The new number isn't divisible by any prime on the list, so the list was incomplete.
- *About 2,300 years old and still one of the most elegant proofs.*
> Primes thin out, but predictably

explain 4 | The prime number theorem
- Near a large number N, about 1 in every ln N numbers is prime.
- Near a million, about 1 in 14. Near a billion, about 1 in 21.
- *Gauss guessed this as a teenager from tables.*
> The largest known primes

quiz 3 | Most of the largest known primes are of what form?
* 2ᵖ − 1 (Mersenne primes)
o 10ⁿ + 1
o n! + 1
o p² + 1
= There's a fast test for them (Lucas–Lehmer). The record has over 41 million digits.
= Found by the volunteer GIMPS project.
> Twin primes

explain 4 | Twin primes and the gap problem
- Twin primes differ by 2: (11, 13), (17, 19), (41, 43).
- Are there infinitely many? Unknown.
- *2013: Yitang Zhang proved there are infinitely many prime pairs within 70 million of each other. That bound has since been cut to 246.*
> Goldbach's conjecture

explain 3 | Goldbach's conjecture
- Every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes: 4 = 2 + 2, 28 = 11 + 17.
- Checked by computer up to 4 × 10¹⁸.
- *Still unproved since 1742.*
> Primes keep your money safe

real 4 | Your bank relies on prime numbers
- Encryption like RSA uses the product of two huge primes.
- Multiplying them is fast; finding them from the product is believed to be infeasible for large enough numbers.
- *Ulam spiral: plot integers in a square spiral and the primes form strange diagonal lines.*
> Next: unsolved problems

## mt-open | Unsolved problems | maths | 0 | - | mt-prime
explain 4 | The Collatz conjecture
- Take any positive integer. If even, halve it. If odd, triple it and add 1. Repeat.
- It always seems to reach 1. 27 takes 111 steps and climbs to 9,232 first.
- *Erdős: "Mathematics may not be ready for such problems."*
> The Millennium Prize Problems

explain 3 | Seven million-dollar problems
- In 2000 the Clay Institute offered $1 million each for seven problems, including P vs NP and the Riemann hypothesis.
- One has been solved: the Poincaré conjecture.
- *Grigori Perelman solved it, then turned down both the prize and the Fields Medal.*
> The moving sofa

explain 4 | The moving sofa problem
- What's the largest area of sofa you can move around a right-angled corner in a hallway of width 1?
- Gerver's shape (1992) gives about 2.2195.
- *A proof that it's optimal was announced in 2024 and is being checked.*
> The happy ending problem

story 4 | The happy ending problem
- Esther Klein showed that any 5 points in general position include 4 forming a convex quadrilateral.
- Erdős and Szekeres generalised it; Klein and Szekeres then married.
- *Erdős named it the "happy ending problem".*
> Next: famous numbers

## mt-num | Famous numbers | maths | 0 | - | mt-open
explain 4 | π is irrational and transcendental
- Lambert proved it irrational in 1761. Lindemann proved it transcendental in 1882.
- That settled an ancient question: you can't square the circle with ruler and compass.
- *Over 100 trillion digits have now been computed.*
> e shows up uninvited

real 4 | The secretary problem and 1/e
- Interviewing candidates one at a time, you must accept or reject each on the spot.
- Best strategy: reject the first 37% (1/e), then take the first who beats them all.
- *You pick the best candidate about 37% of the time.* Some apply it to dating, half-jokingly.
> 6174 is a mathematical black hole

story 4 | Kaprekar's constant, 6174
- Take any four-digit number with at least two different digits. Arrange digits high to low, then low to high, subtract.
- Repeat. You always reach 6174 within seven steps.
- *6174: 7641 − 1467 = 6174.*
> Graham's number

explain 4 | Graham's number
- An upper bound from a 1970s problem about colouring hypercubes. It's so large the observable universe can't hold its digits.
- It needs special notation (Knuth's up-arrows) even to describe.
- *Yet we know its last digits: it ends in …7262464195387.*
> 1729 is a taxicab number

story 4 | 1729: "a very interesting number"
- Hardy visited Ramanujan in hospital in a taxi numbered 1729 and called it dull.
- Ramanujan replied it's the smallest number expressible as the sum of two cubes in two ways: 1³ + 12³ = 9³ + 10³.
- *Numbers like it are now called taxicab numbers.*
> Next: geometry surprises

## mt-geo | Geometry and topology surprises | maths | 0 | - | mt-num
explain 4 | A Möbius strip has one side and one edge
- Take a strip of paper, give it a half-twist, tape the ends.
- Draw a line along the middle: it covers both "sides" before returning.
- *Cut it along the middle and it stays in one piece, twice as long.*
> A coffee mug is a doughnut

explain 3 | To a topologist, a mug is a doughnut
- Topology ignores stretching and bending, but not tearing or gluing.
- A mug and a doughnut each have exactly one hole, so they're equivalent.
- *A topologist can't tell their coffee mug from their breakfast.*
> The four colour theorem needed a computer

story 4 | The four colour theorem
- Any map can be coloured with four colours so no neighbouring regions match.
- Proved in 1976 by Appel and Haken with over 1,000 hours of computer checking.
- *The first major theorem proved with essential computer help; many mathematicians were uneasy.*
> Banach–Tarski

story 5 | Banach–Tarski: one ball becomes two
- Using the axiom of choice, a solid ball can be cut into finitely many pieces and reassembled into two balls the same size as the original.
- The pieces are so bizarre they have no well-defined volume.
- *It's a theorem, not a trick. Physical matter can't be cut that way.*
> Spheres turned inside out

story 4 | You can turn a sphere inside out
- Smale proved in 1957 that a sphere can be everted (turned inside out) if surfaces may pass through themselves, without creases.
- Nobody could picture how at first.
- *Bernard Morin, who was blind, helped work out an explicit way.*
> Maths curiosities complete
